<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <model name='sentinel_vision_slung'>
    <include merge='true'>
      <uri>sentinel</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- Slung load: ONE rigid body (rod + payload welded together) on a SINGLE ball joint from
         base_link—a simple pendulum, not a double pendulum. Earlier this had the rod and
         payload as two separate links on two ball joints in series (independent rotation at
         each end), which let the rod and payload swing independently of each other—a real
         second degree of freedom a taut string physically can't have (a string is inextensible
         and has no bending stiffness, so it just goes taut and the whole thing swings as one
         mass). That extra DOF was almost certainly why the payload was swinging out to ~90
         degrees—genuine double-pendulum energy exchange, not just insufficient damping.

         Combined mass/CG/inertia (COMBINED_MASS_KG, COMBINED_CG_Z, COMBINED_IXX/IZZ) are
         computed in generate-slung-load-model.py via the parallel-axis theorem from the rod's
         and payload's own geometry—ROD_LOCAL_Z/PAYLOAD_LOCAL_Z below are each visual's offset
         from the combined CG (the link's own origin), not from base_link.

         No <collision> on this link (visual only), matching the earlier reference model's choice
         to isolate pendulum dynamics from the contact solver—revisit only if we actually want
         to model the load hitting something. Since there's no collision, spawning below ground
         level (e.g. QUAD1_LOCATION z=0) is visually odd but not a physics problem—no contact
         solver reaction, since there's nothing here to generate contact with.

         Attach point (ATTACH_X/Y/Z_M) is the center of one landing gear's horizontal foot pad
         (base_link_collision_3 in sentinel_base/model.sdf, i.e. one arm of the inverted-T leg),
         not base_link's own origin—the legs aren't separate links/joints in this model, just
         collision boxes glued onto base_link, so this is purely a different offset on the same
         rigid body, not a joint to a different body.

         Damping is real aerodynamic quadratic drag via the gz-sim Hydrodynamics system plugin
         (force = -0.5 * air_density * Cd * A * v*|v|, per axis—see the xUabsU-style
         parameters below), NOT SDF's <velocity_decay>, which is confirmed unimplemented/silently
         ignored by gz-physics's DART backend as of Harmonic (gz-physics#635)—setting it had
         zero effect at any magnitude, hence its absence here. AIR_DENSITY_KG_M3 is deliberately
         inflated well past real air in generate-slung-load-model.py—see the comment there for
         why (short version: the real hardware's fast decay is almost certainly dominated by
         liquid sloshing inside the bottle, a mechanism this rigid-body model can't represent
         directly, so air density is repurposed as a single empirical damping knob instead).
         Rotational (roll/pitch/yaw) damping is a rough strip-theory estimate, not rigorously
         derived like the translational terms—retune if payload spin specifically looks wrong.
         Added-mass terms (xDotU etc.) are left at 0: negligible for a small solid body in air
         (they matter for displacement-scale bodies in water, which is what this plugin was built
         for).

         GENERATED FILE—edit generate-slung-load-model.py and re-run it, don't hand-edit this
         output (model.sdf) directly, your changes will be overwritten. -->

    <link name="slung_load_link">
      <pose relative_to="base_link">{ATTACH_X_M} {ATTACH_Y_M} {COMBINED_CG_Z} 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>{COMBINED_MASS_KG}</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>{COMBINED_IXX}</ixx><iyy>{COMBINED_IXX}</iyy><izz>{COMBINED_IZZ}</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="rod_visual">
        <pose>0 0 {ROD_LOCAL_Z} 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>{ROD_RADIUS_M}</radius>
            <length>{ROD_LENGTH_M}</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.55 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.55 0.55 0.55 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="slung_load_visual">
        <pose>0 0 {PAYLOAD_LOCAL_Z} 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>{PAYLOAD_RADIUS_M}</radius>
            <length>{PAYLOAD_HEIGHT_M}</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.3 0.8 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.3 0.8 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- Spin marker: a bright stripe running the full height of the payload cylinder, offset
           to one side. Purely visual, no collision/inertial effect. If it stays pointing the
           same absolute direction while the payload moves around its orbit, that's pure
           revolution (no self-spin). If it visibly rotates in place, that's real spin about the
           payload's own long axis (the nRabsR term below, the one whose drag coefficient is the
           least trustworthy of the three—see the comment above the Hydrodynamics plugin). -->
      <visual name="slung_load_spin_marker">
        <pose>{STRIPE_OFFSET_M} 0 {PAYLOAD_LOCAL_Z} 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>{STRIPE_THICKNESS_M} {STRIPE_WIDTH_M} {PAYLOAD_HEIGHT_M}</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1.0 0.55 0.0 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1.0 0.55 0.0 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <joint name="slung_load_pivot" type="ball">
      <pose relative_to="base_link">{ATTACH_X_M} {ATTACH_Y_M} {ATTACH_Z_M} 0 0 0</pose>
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>slung_load_link</child>
    </joint>

    <plugin
      filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system"
      name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher">
      <dimensions>3</dimensions>
      <odom_publish_frequency>250</odom_publish_frequency>
      <!-- Left disabled: the vision_odometry_noise ROS 2 node owns all noise/jitter
           injection (per sensor_noise_profile.json), not this plugin. -->
      <!-- <gaussian_noise>0.1</gaussian_noise> -->
    </plugin>

    <!-- Real aerodynamic quadratic drag (Fossen model, force = coeff * v*|v| per axis), derived
         from the rod's and payload's combined crossflow/end-cap area in air—see
         generate-slung-load-model.py. Verified parameter names against
         /usr/share/gz/gz-sim8/worlds/auv_controls.sdf on this host (an installed gz-sim demo
         world), not guessed from memory.

         X_UABSU/Y_VABSV/Z_WABSW (translational) are the sum of the rod's and payload's
         individually-derived drag—valid since both surfaces move at the same velocity now
         (one rigid body), so their drag force contributions add directly. K_PABSP/M_QABSQ/
         N_RABSR (rotational) are summed the same way as a pragmatic approximation; strictly the
         combined body's rotational drag about this joint isn't just the sum of each part's drag
         about its own center, but ROTATIONAL_DRAG_MULTIPLIER was already an order-of-magnitude
         fudge factor before this, not a rigorously derived term. -->
    <plugin
      filename="gz-sim-hydrodynamics-system"
      name="gz::sim::systems::Hydrodynamics">
      <link_name>slung_load_link</link_name>
      <xDotU>0</xDotU><yDotV>0</yDotV><zDotW>0</zDotW>
      <kDotP>0</kDotP><mDotQ>0</mDotQ><nDotR>0</nDotR>
      <xUabsU>{X_UABSU}</xUabsU><xU>0</xU>
      <yVabsV>{Y_VABSV}</yVabsV><yV>0</yV>
      <zWabsW>{Z_WABSW}</zWabsW><zW>0</zW>
      <kPabsP>{K_PABSP}</kPabsP><kP>0</kP>
      <mQabsQ>{M_QABSQ}</mQabsQ><mQ>0</mQ>
      <nRabsR>{N_RABSR}</nRabsR><nR>0</nR>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>
